= Solution
{parent=3/a}
{c}

For a regular <parametrized surface> that covers its image once, up to sets of zero <area>, its <surface area> is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Area}(S)=\iint_U\left\|\frac{\partial\mathbf r}{\partial u}\times\frac{\partial\mathbf r}{\partial v}\right\|\,du\,dv.}
$$
The integrand is the <area element of a surface>, the <area> of the small <parallelogram> spanned by the two <tangent vectors>. A <parametrization> that covers a region more than once counts that multiplicity; the geometric <surface area> requires a single covering or a division into nonoverlapping patches.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}
{c}

= i
{parent=3/b}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=3/b/i}
{c}

Interpolate the <radius> and height by
$$
\rho(t)=a+(c-a)t,\qquad z(t)=b+(d-b)t,\qquad 0\leq t\leq1.
$$
A <parametrized surface> for the <conical frustum> is
$$
\boxed{\mathbf R(t,\theta)=\bigl(\rho(t)\cos\theta,\rho(t)\sin\theta,b+(d-b)t\bigr),\quad 0\leq t\leq1,\quad0\leq\theta<2\pi.}
$$
The strict positivity of $a,c$ gives $\rho(t)>0$. Since $d>b$, the height identifies $t$ uniquely; the <angle> then identifies a <point> of each <circle> uniquely modulo $2\pi$. Hence this covers the lateral <surface of revolution> once. If $a=c$, it is the lateral surface of a <circular cylinder>.

= ii
{parent=3/b}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=3/b/ii}
{c}

For the <parametrized surface> in <3/b/i>,
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf R_t&=((c-a)\cos\theta,(c-a)\sin\theta,d-b),\\
\mathbf R_\theta&=(-\rho\sin\theta,\rho\cos\theta,0).
\end{aligned}
$$
These <tangent vectors> are <orthogonal>, and their <Euclidean norms> are $\sqrt{(c-a)^2+(d-b)^2}$ and $\rho$, respectively. Their <cross product> therefore has <Euclidean norm> $\rho\sqrt{(c-a)^2+(d-b)^2}$. Integration gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Area}(\Sigma_1)
&=2\pi\sqrt{(c-a)^2+(d-b)^2}\int_0^1(a+(c-a)t)\,dt\\
&=\boxed{\pi(a+c)\sqrt{(a-c)^2+(b-d)^2}.}
\end{aligned}
$$
This is the lateral <surface area> of a <conical frustum>, with no end disks included.

= iii
{parent=3/b}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=3/b/iii}
{c}

The endpoint <radii> are $5$ and $13$, but **substituting those two numbers in the finite <conical frustum> formula is invalid**. A rotation about the $z$-axis preserves the horizontal <angle> between the endpoint <position vectors>; here $(3,4)$ and $(12,5)$ are not parallel. The <radius> along the generating segment is not linear in height.

Parametrize the actual segment by
$$
(x,y,z)=(3+9t,4+t,1+6t),\qquad0\leq t\leq1.
$$
Its rotated <circle> at height $1+6t$ has <radius>
$$
\rho(t)=\sqrt{(3+9t)^2+(4+t)^2}=\sqrt{25+62t+82t^2},\qquad
\rho'(t)=\frac{31+82t}{\rho(t)}.
$$
Thus the rotated image is the <surface of revolution> $\mathbf R(t,\theta)=(\rho(t)\cos\theta,\rho(t)\sin\theta,1+6t)$. Applying the <conical frustum> formula to infinitesimal pieces of this radial profile, or directly using the <cross product> as in <3/b/ii>, gives the <surface area of a surface of revolution>
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal A
&=2\pi\int_0^1\rho(t)\sqrt{\rho'(t)^2+36}\,dt\\
&=2\pi\int_0^1\sqrt{9676t^2+7316t+1861}\,dt.
\end{aligned}
$$
To evaluate it explicitly, put $u=82t+31$. Then $9676t^2+7316t+1861=(59u^2+19602)/41$. Define
$$
G(u)=u\sqrt{59u^2+19602}+\frac{19602}{\sqrt{59}}\log\bigl(\sqrt{59}\,u+\sqrt{59u^2+19602}\bigr).
$$
The <chain rule> gives $G'(u)=2\sqrt{59u^2+19602}$, so the answer is
$$
\boxed{\mathcal A=\frac{\pi}{82\sqrt{41}}\bigl(G(113)-G(31)\bigr)\simeq561.8721076764.}
$$
The positive <logarithm> arguments make this a real-valued expression. Rotating the segment's endpoints independently into one vertical <plane> before joining them would instead create a different surface, with <surface area> $180\pi\simeq565.4867$.

\Image[mathematics-iv-q3-swept-segment.png]
{title=The rotated segment has a curved radial profile, although its three-dimensional generator is straight.}

= c
{parent=3}
